On Apr 21, 2014, at 10:02 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:

> I wanted to add a to_vector function for situations where you had an array, like
>
> parameters {
>   matrix[5,4] theta[3];
> }
> model {
>   to_vector(theta) ~ normal(0.0,1.0);
> }
>
> So, I wrote this
>
>     template <typename T, int R1, int R2>
>     inline
>     Eigen::Matrix<T,Eigen::Dynamic,1>
>     to_vector(const std::vector< Eigen::Matrix<T,R1,R2> >& m) {
>       std::cout << "Size = " << m.size() <<
>                    " Rows = " << m[0].rows() <<
>                    " Cols = " << m[0].cols() << std::endl;
>       return Eigen::Matrix<T,Eigen::Dynamic,1>::Map(m.data()->data(),
>              m.size()*m[0].rows()*m[0].cols());
>     }
>
> which promptly segfaulted
>
> vec_theta =
> Size = 3 Rows = 5 Cols = 4
> Segmentation fault
>
> which made me look up
>
> http://eigen.tuxfamily.org/dox/group__TopicStlContainers.html
>
> which only refers to fixed-size types but it appears as if there is some sort of an alignment problem. I guess a std::vector of Eigen::Matrix puts the each individual Eigen::Matrix in contiguous memory locations but does not necessarily but them adjacent to each other.

Correct.  Each std::vector does its own malloc.  It uses
the RAII pattern (resource acquisition is initialization) pattern:

  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resource_Acquisition_Is_Initialization

The Eigen::Matrix class uses contigous memory, so any matrix object
has contiguous memory.

>
> Does anyone know of a better way to do it than
>
>     template <typename T, int R1, int R2>
>     inline
>     Eigen::Matrix<T,Eigen::Dynamic,1>
>     to_vector(const std::vector< Eigen::Matrix<T,R1,R2> >& m) {
>       int mat_size = m[0].rows()*m[0].cols();
>       Eigen::Matrix<T,Eigen::Dynamic,1> vec(m.size() * mat_size);
>       int pos = 0;
>       for(unsigned int i = 0; i < m.size(); i++) {
>         for(int k = 0; k < m[0].cols(); k++) {
>           for(int j = 0; j < m[0].rows(); j++) {
>             vec(pos++) = m[i](j,k);
>           }
>         }
>       }
>       return vec;
>     }
>
> Also, how do you specify the function signatures so that it would work with any multidimensional array of matrices like
>
> matrix[5,4] theta[a,b,c,d,e,f];

What you have to do here is write recursive template programs that
peels off one std::vector at a time.  And it needs some auxiliary
template functions for calculating size_of using a similar trick and
a template metaprogram for peeling out the scalar type of an object.
They go roughly like this:

For scalar_type_of:

  template <typename T>
  class scalar_type_of {
    typedef T type;
  };
  template <typename T>
  class scalar_type_of<vector<T> > {
    typedef typename scalar_type_of<T>::type type;
  }
  template <typename T, int R, int C>
  class scalar_type_of<Matrix<T,Dynamic,Dynamic> > {
    typedef T type;
  }
  ... + (Dynamic,1) and (1,Dynamic) specializations ...

And then for size_of:

  template <typename T>
  int size_of(const T& x) {
    return 1;
  }
  template <typename T>
  int size_of(const vector<T>& x) {
    return x.size() == 0U
      ? 0
      : static_cast<int>(x.size()) * size_of(x[0]);
  }
  template <typename T, int R, int C>
  int size_of(const Matrix<T,R,C>& x) {
    return x.size();
  }

And then down to business:

   template <typename T1, typename T2>
   Matrix<typename scalar_type_of<T>::type,Dynamic,1>
   to_vector(const T& x) {
     Matrix<typename scalar_type_of<T>::type,Dynamic,1> result(size_of(x));
     int pos = 0;
     to_vector(x,result,pos);
   }

   template <typename T>
   T to_vector(const T& x,
               Matrix<T,Dynamic,1>& result,
               int& pos) {
     result[pos++] = x;
   }
   template <typename T>
   int to_vector(const std::vector<T>& x,
                 Matrix<T,Dynamic,1>& result,
                 int& pos) {
     for (size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i)
       to_vector(x,result[i],pos);
   }
   template <typename T, int R, int C>
   int to_vector(const Matrix<T,R,C>& x,
                 Matrix<T,Dynamic,1>& result,
                 int& pos) {
     for (int i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i)
       result[pos++] = x(i);
   }

- Bob
